-- The 2023 Merger Guidelines, sections 2.2 to 2.11 (Guidelines 2 to 11).
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW: each result says what the Agencies examine, infer or conclude.
--
-- Where the text gives a test (two elements joined by "and", a share over 50%, a trend that "could magnify" the concerns
-- of a named Guideline), it is encoded as the test. Where it lists indicators and gives no way of combining them, the caller
-- supplies the indicators the evidence shows, and the result reports them back; nothing here weighs them. A BOOLEAN
-- input is the caller's own finding on the evidence the text describes.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mg-nouns`
IMPORT `mg-concentration`

§ `2. Applying the Merger Guidelines: Guidelines 2 to 11`

§§ `2.2 Guideline 2: Mergers Can Violate the Law When They Eliminate Substantial Competition Between Firms`

-- [pdf pages 7 and 8 of 51]: "If evidence demonstrates substantial competition between the merging parties prior to the
-- merger, that ordinarily suggests that the merger may substantially lessen competition." The indicators ([pdf pages 8
-- and 9 of 51]) are "for example" and carry no test, so they are reported, not weighed.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.2 [pdf pages 7 to 9 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 2 facts`
GIVETH A `Guideline 2 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 2 of` MEANS `Guideline 2 finding` WITH
    `Guideline 2 indicators present` IS facts's `the indicators the evidence shows`
    `the Agencies ordinarily take it that the merger may substantially lessen competition` IS facts's `the evidence demonstrates substantial competition between the merging parties`

§§ `2.3 Guideline 3: Mergers Can Violate the Law When They Increase the Risk of Coordination`

-- 2.3.A "Highly Concentrated Market" ([pdf page 9 of 51]): "Markets that are highly concentrated after a merger that
-- significantly increases concentration (see Guideline 1) are presumptively susceptible to coordination." Read as the
-- HHI limb of Guideline 1's table (fork G3-1).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.3.A [pdf page 9 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 3 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the market is presumptively susceptible to coordination, under 2.3.A` IF
    `the HHI limb of the presumption is met by` (facts's `post-merger HHI of the market`) (facts's `change in HHI of the market`)

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.3.A [pdf pages 9 and 10 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 3 facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Guideline 3 primary factor`
`the Guideline 3 primary factors in` MEANS
    append (IF `the market is presumptively susceptible to coordination, under 2.3.A` facts
            THEN LIST `highly concentrated market after a merger that significantly increases concentration`
            ELSE EMPTY)
           (append (IF facts's `firms with a substantial share appear to have coordinated, or attempted to, before`
                    THEN LIST `prior actual or attempted coordination`
                    ELSE EMPTY)
                   (IF facts's `the merger eliminates a maverick or significantly changes its incentives`
                    THEN LIST `elimination of a maverick`
                    ELSE EMPTY))

-- Section 1 ([pdf page 4 of 51]): "A market that is highly concentrated or has seen prior anticompetitive coordination is
-- inherently vulnerable and the Agencies will infer, subject to rebuttal evidence, that the merger may substantially
-- lessen competition. In a market that is not highly concentrated, the Agencies investigate whether facts suggest a
-- greater risk of coordination than market structure alone would suggest."
-- 2.3.A ([pdf page 9 of 51]): "The Agencies may conclude that post-merger market conditions are susceptible to
-- coordinated interaction and that the merger materially increases the risk of coordination if any of the three primary
-- factors are present."
-- 2.3.B ([pdf page 10 of 51]): secondary factors may show the risk "even absent the primary risk factors. Not all
-- secondary factors must be present": reported, not weighed.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 1 and sections 2.3, 2.3.A, 2.3.B [pdf pages 4, 9 to 11 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 3 facts`
GIVETH A `Guideline 3 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 3 of` MEANS `Guideline 3 finding` WITH
    `Guideline 3 primary factors present` IS `the Guideline 3 primary factors in` facts
    `Guideline 3 secondary factors present` IS facts's `secondary factors the evidence shows`
    `response factors present` IS facts's `response factors the evidence shows`
    `the market is presumptively susceptible to coordination` IS `the market is presumptively susceptible to coordination, under 2.3.A` facts
    `the Agencies infer, subject to rebuttal, that the merger may substantially lessen competition` IS
            `the market is presumptively susceptible to coordination, under 2.3.A` facts
         OR facts's `firms with a substantial share appear to have coordinated, or attempted to, before`
    `the Agencies may conclude the merger materially increases the risk of coordination` IS NOT (null (`the Guideline 3 primary factors in` facts))
    `the Agencies investigate whether facts suggest a greater risk than structure alone` IS NOT (`highly concentrated, by an HHI of` (facts's `post-merger HHI of the market`))

§§ `2.4 Guideline 4: Mergers Can Violate the Law When They Eliminate a Potential Entrant in a Concentrated Market`

-- Footnote 21 ([pdf page 11 of 51]): "A concentrated market is one with an HHI greater than 1,000". Footnote 23 ([pdf
-- page 12 of 51]): where the market is not yet commercialized, "Where there are few equivalent potential entrants,
-- including one or both of the merging firms, that indicates that the future market, once commercialized, will be
-- concentrated."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.4, footnotes 21 and 23 [pdf pages 11 and 12 of 51]
GIVEN m IS A `Concentration of the market entered`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the market entered is concentrated, for Guideline 4` MEANS
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN `a market whose HHI is` h THEN `concentrated, by an HHI of` h
    WHEN `a future market not yet commercialized` few THEN few

GIVEN m IS A `Concentration of the market entered`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the market entered is highly concentrated, for Guideline 4` MEANS
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN `a market whose HHI is` h THEN `highly concentrated, by an HHI of` h
    WHEN `a future market not yet commercialized` few THEN FALSE

-- 2.4.A ([pdf page 12 of 51]): "If the merging firm had a reasonable probability of entering a highly concentrated
-- relevant market, this suggests benefits that would have resulted from its entry would be competitively significant,
-- unless there is substantial direct evidence that the competitive effect would be de minimis." (fork G4-2)
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.4.A [pdf page 12 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 4 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the second element of 2.4.A is suggested by high concentration` IF
        `the market entered is highly concentrated, for Guideline 4` (facts's `the market entered`)
    AND facts's `a merging firm had a reasonable probability of entering other than through an anticompetitive merger`
    AND NOT (facts's `there is substantial direct evidence that the effect of that entry would be de minimis`)

-- 2.4.A ([pdf page 12 of 51]): "the Agencies examine (1) whether one or both of the merging firms had a reasonable
-- probability of entering the relevant market other than through an anticompetitive merger, and (2) whether such entry
-- offered a substantial likelihood of ultimately producing deconcentration of the market or other significant
-- procompetitive effects."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.4.A [pdf page 12 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 4 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `actual potential competition: the elements of 2.4.A are met` IF
        `the market entered is concentrated, for Guideline 4` (facts's `the market entered`)
    AND facts's `a merging firm had a reasonable probability of entering other than through an anticompetitive merger`
    AND (    facts's `that entry offered a substantial likelihood of deconcentration or other significant procompetitive effects`
          OR `the second element of 2.4.A is suggested by high concentration` facts)

-- 2.4.B ([pdf page 13 of 51]): "the Agencies consider whether a current market participant could reasonably consider one
-- of the merging companies to be a potential entrant and whether that potential entrant has a likely influence on
-- existing competition."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.4.B [pdf page 13 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 4 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perceived potential competition: the elements of 2.4.B are met` IF
        `the market entered is concentrated, for Guideline 4` (facts's `the market entered`)
    AND facts's `a current participant could reasonably consider a merging firm a potential entrant`
    AND facts's `that potential entrant has a likely influence on existing competition`

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 2.4, 2.4.A, 2.4.B [pdf pages 11 to 13 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 4 facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Guideline 4 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 4 of` MEANS
    IF `the HHI given is impossible` (facts's `the market entered`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`Guideline 4 finding` WITH
        `the market entered is concentrated` IS `the market entered is concentrated, for Guideline 4` (facts's `the market entered`)
        `the second element is suggested by high concentration` IS `the second element of 2.4.A is suggested by high concentration` facts
        `actual potential competition: the merger may substantially lessen competition` IS `actual potential competition: the elements of 2.4.A are met` facts
        `perceived potential competition: the merger may substantially lessen competition` IS `perceived potential competition: the elements of 2.4.B are met` facts
        `entry evidence present` IS facts's `entry evidence the record shows`
        `influence evidence present` IS facts's `influence evidence the record shows`)
    WHERE
        GIVEN m IS A `Concentration of the market entered`
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        `the HHI given is impossible` MEANS
            CONSIDER m
            WHEN `a market whose HHI is` h THEN h LESS THAN 0 OR h GREATER THAN 10000
            WHEN `a future market not yet commercialized` few THEN FALSE

§§ `2.5 Guideline 5: Mergers Can Violate the Law When They Create a Firm that May Limit Access to Products or Services That Its Rivals Use to Compete`

-- Footnote 30 ([pdf page 17 of 51]): "The Agencies will generally infer, in the absence of countervailing evidence, that
-- the merging firm has or is approaching monopoly power in the related product if it has a share greater than 50% of
-- the related product market. A merger involving a related product with share of less than 50% may still substantially
-- lessen competition". The share is the foreclosure share of 2.5.A.2 (fork G5-1); exactly 50% is in neither sentence.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.5.A.2, footnote 30 [pdf page 17 of 51]
GIVEN share IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      countervailing IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What footnote 30 says of the foreclosure share`
`what footnote 30 says of a foreclosure share of` MEANS
    CONSIDER share
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `no related market was defined around the related product`
    WHEN JUST s THEN
        BRANCH
            IF s GREATER THAN 50 AND NOT countervailing THEN `the Agencies generally infer the merged firm has or approaches monopoly power over the related product`
            IF s GREATER THAN 50                        THEN `the share is over fifty percent, but countervailing evidence displaces the inference`
            IF s LESS THAN 50                           THEN `no inference from the share, and the merger may still substantially lessen competition`
            OTHERWISE                                        `no inference from the share, and footnote 30 says nothing of a share of exactly fifty percent`

-- 2.5.A.2 "Structure of the Related Market" ([pdf pages 16 and 17 of 51]): "If the share or other evidence show that the
-- merged firm is approaching or has monopoly power over the related product, and the related product is competitively
-- significant, those factors alone are a sufficient basis to demonstrate that the dependent firms do not have adequate
-- substitutes and the merged firm has the ability to weaken or exclude them". This shows the ABILITY only.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.5.A.2 [pdf pages 16 and 17 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 5 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `market structure shows the merged firm's ability to weaken or exclude dependent rivals` IF
        (    `what footnote 30 says of a foreclosure share of` (facts's `the foreclosure share`) (facts's `there is countervailing evidence on monopoly power over the related product`)
                 EQUALS `the Agencies generally infer the merged firm has or approaches monopoly power over the related product`
          OR facts's `other evidence shows the merged firm has or approaches monopoly power over the related product`)
    AND facts's `the related product is competitively significant`

-- 2.5.A ([pdf page 14 of 51]): the merger "may substantially lessen competition when the merged firm has both the ability
-- and incentive to limit access to the related product so as to weaken or exclude some of its rivals".
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.5.A [pdf page 14 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 5 facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the merged firm has the ability to limit access` IF
        `market structure shows the merged firm's ability to weaken or exclude dependent rivals` facts
    OR facts's `the merged firm's ability to limit access is otherwise shown`

-- 2.5.B ([pdf page 18 of 51]): "If rivals would continue to access or purchase a related product controlled by the merged
-- firm post-merger, the merger can substantially lessen competition if the merged firm would gain or increase visibility
-- into rivals' competitively sensitive information."
-- 2.5.C ([pdf pages 18 and 19 of 51]): ability and incentive, or visibility, "may create entry barriers"; and the
-- deterrence "may occur irrespective of the merged firm's incentive to limit access" (fork G5-3).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 2.5, 2.5.A, 2.5.A.1, 2.5.A.2, 2.5.B, 2.5.C [pdf pages 13 to 19 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 5 facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Guideline 5 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 5 of` MEANS
    IF `the share given is impossible` (facts's `the foreclosure share`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`Guideline 5 finding` WITH
        `the share inference` IS `what footnote 30 says of a foreclosure share of` (facts's `the foreclosure share`) (facts's `there is countervailing evidence on monopoly power over the related product`)
        `market structure shows the ability to weaken or exclude dependent rivals` IS `market structure shows the merged firm's ability to weaken or exclude dependent rivals` facts
        `(A) the merger may substantially lessen competition by limiting access` IS
                `the merged firm has the ability to limit access` facts
            AND facts's `the merged firm has the incentive to limit access`
        `(B) the merger may substantially lessen competition through visibility into rivals' information` IS
                facts's `rivals would continue to access the related product after the merger`
            AND facts's `the merged firm would gain or increase visibility into rivals' competitively sensitive information`
        `(C) the merger may create barriers that deter rivals from entering or investing` IS
                `the merged firm has the ability to limit access` facts
            OR facts's `the merged firm would gain or increase visibility into rivals' competitively sensitive information`
        `Guideline 5 considerations present` IS facts's `considerations the evidence shows`)
    WHERE
        GIVEN share IS A MAYBE NUMBER
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        `the share given is impossible` MEANS
            CONSIDER share
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST s THEN s LESS THAN 0 OR s GREATER THAN 100

§§ `2.6 Guideline 6: Mergers Can Violate the Law When They Entrench or Extend a Dominant Position`

-- [pdf page 19 of 51]: "the Agencies first assess whether one of the merging firms has a dominant position based on direct
-- evidence or market shares showing durable market power." The text gives no share figure for dominance, so dominance is
-- the caller's finding; the Agencies then examine whether the merger may entrench it (2.6.A) or extend it (2.6.B).
-- Footnote 37 ([pdf page 21 of 51]): "The Agencies assess acquisitions of nascent competitive threats by non-dominant
-- firms under the other Guidelines."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 2.6, 2.6.A, 2.6.B, footnote 37 [pdf pages 19 to 22 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 6 facts`
GIVETH A `Guideline 6 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 6 of` MEANS `Guideline 6 finding` WITH
    `the Agencies examine the merger as entrenching or extending a dominant position` IS
            facts's `a merging firm has a dominant position, shown by direct evidence or by shares showing durable market power`
        AND (facts's `the merger may entrench that position` OR facts's `the merger may extend that position into another market`)
    `the nascent threat is assessed` IS
        BRANCH
            IF NOT (facts's `the acquired firm is a nascent competitive threat`) THEN `no nascent competitive threat is acquired`
            IF facts's `a merging firm has a dominant position, shown by direct evidence or by shares showing durable market power` THEN `under Guideline 6, the acquirer being dominant`
            OTHERWISE `under the other Guidelines, the acquirer not being dominant`
    `ways of entrenching present` IS facts's `ways of entrenching the evidence shows`
    `ways of extending present` IS facts's `ways of extending the evidence shows`

§§ `2.7 Guideline 7: When an Industry Undergoes a Trend Toward Consolidation`

-- [pdf pages 23 and 24 of 51]. "Trend Toward Concentration": "the Agencies identify a stronger presumption of harm from
-- undue concentration (see Guideline 1), and a greater risk of substantially lessening competition when a merger
-- eliminates competition between the merging parties (see Guideline 2) or increases the risk of coordination (see
-- Guideline 3)". "Trend Toward Vertical Integration": "could magnify the concerns discussed in Guideline 5". "Arms Race
-- for Bargaining Leverage": "This can exacerbate the problems discussed in Guidelines 1-6". "Multiple Mergers": "the
-- Agencies may examine multiple deals in light of the combined trend toward concentration".
-- The two result names `magnified concerns under Guideline 5` and `exacerbated problems under Guidelines 1 to 6` drop the
-- text's modal: a trend toward vertical integration COULD magnify the concerns, and an arms race CAN exacerbate the
-- problems. Neither result says the concerns are magnified or the problems exacerbated in a given case.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.7 [pdf pages 23 and 24 of 51]
@desc What the Guidelines say each trend does to the analysis; a trend toward vertical integration "could magnify" Guideline 5's concerns, and an arms race "can exacerbate" the problems of Guidelines 1 to 6.
GIVEN trend IS AN `Industry trend`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What a trend does to the analysis`
`what Guideline 7 says the trend does` MEANS
    CONSIDER trend
    WHEN `trend toward concentration`             THEN LIST `a stronger presumption of harm under Guideline 1`, `a greater risk under Guideline 2`, `a greater risk under Guideline 3`
    WHEN `trend toward vertical integration`      THEN LIST `magnified concerns under Guideline 5`
    WHEN `arms race for bargaining leverage`      THEN LIST `exacerbated problems under Guidelines 1 to 6`
    WHEN `multiple mergers in the same industry`  THEN LIST `the deals may be examined together, in light of the combined trend`

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.7 [pdf pages 23 and 24 of 51]
GIVEN trends IS A LIST OF `Industry trend`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What a trend does to the analysis`
`what Guideline 7 says the trends do` MEANS concat (map (GIVEN t YIELD `what Guideline 7 says the trend does` t) trends)

§§ `2.8 Guideline 8: When a Merger is Part of a Series of Multiple Acquisitions`

-- [pdf page 24 of 51]: "the Agencies may evaluate the series of acquisitions as part of an industry trend (see Guideline 7)
-- or evaluate the overall pattern or strategy of serial acquisitions by the acquiring firm collectively under Guidelines
-- 1-6."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.8 [pdf page 24 of 51]
GIVEN `a pattern or strategy of multiple acquisitions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `How a series of acquisitions is examined`
`how Guideline 8 examines the series` MEANS
    IF `a pattern or strategy of multiple acquisitions`
    THEN LIST `as part of an industry trend under Guideline 7`, `as a pattern or strategy, collectively, under Guidelines 1 to 6`
    ELSE EMPTY

-- "Collectively under Guidelines 1-6", for Guideline 1: the firms of the series are treated as the parties to one merger,
-- on the shares the market had before the first acquisition of the series. Fork G8-1: the text gives no arithmetic for
-- "collectively"; this is the reading taken, assumed, not ruled.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.8 and section 2.1 [pdf pages 24, 6 and 7 of 51]
GIVEN `the market before the series` IS A LIST OF `Firm in the relevant market`
      `the firms in the series` IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `Guideline 1 finding`
`Guideline 1 applied to the whole series` MEANS
    `the Agencies' reading under Guideline 1 of`
        (map (GIVEN f YIELD `Firm in the relevant market` WITH
                  `the firm's name` IS f's `the firm's name`
                  `the firm's pre-merger share` IS f's `the firm's pre-merger share`
                  `the firm is a party to the merger` IS elem (f's `the firm's name`) `the firms in the series`)
             `the market before the series`)

§§ `2.9 Guideline 9: When a Merger Involves a Multi-Sided Platform`

-- [pdf pages 24 to 27 of 51]: "the Agencies consider competition between platforms, competition on a platform, and
-- competition to displace the platform." Scenarios A to D ([pdf page 26 of 51]) are mergers that bear on competition
-- between platforms; "When a merger involves a platform operator and platform participants, the Agencies carefully
-- examine whether the merger would create conflicts of interest" (competition on a platform); "The Agencies protect
-- competition to displace the platform or any of its services" ([pdf page 27 of 51]).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.9 [pdf pages 24 to 27 of 51]
GIVEN involves IS A `Platform merger`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Kind of platform competition`
`the platform competition Guideline 9 examines` MEANS
    CONSIDER involves
    WHEN `two platform operators` THEN LIST `competition between platforms`
    WHEN `a platform operator acquiring a platform participant` THEN LIST `competition between platforms`, `competition on a platform`
    WHEN `an acquisition of a service that facilitates participation on multiple platforms` THEN LIST `competition between platforms`
    WHEN `an acquisition of a firm providing other important inputs to platform services` THEN LIST `competition between platforms`
    WHEN `a platform operator that is also a participant merging with a firm whose product is offered on the platform` THEN LIST `competition on a platform`, `competition between platforms`, `competition to displace a platform`
    WHEN `a merger affecting a firm or service that could displace the platform or one of its services` THEN LIST `competition to displace a platform`

-- [pdf page 25 of 51]: the Agencies will seek to prohibit a merger that harms competition "within a relevant market for any
-- product or service offered on a platform to any group of participants—i.e., around one side of the platform".
-- Footnote 47: "In the limited scenario of a 'special type of two-sided platform known as a "transaction" platform,'
-- under Section 1 of the Sherman Act, a relevant market encompassing both sides of a two-sided platform may be
-- warranted", limited to platforms that "cannot make a sale to one side of the platform without simultaneously making
-- a sale to the other."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.9, footnote 47 [pdf page 25 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Platform facts`
GIVETH A `Platform finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 9 of` MEANS `Platform finding` WITH
    `the competition examined` IS `the platform competition Guideline 9 examines` (facts's `what the merger involves`)
    `a relevant market around one side of the platform is available` IS TRUE
    `a relevant market encompassing both sides may be warranted, under Section 1 of the Sherman Act` IS facts's `the platform cannot make a sale to one side without simultaneously making a sale to the other`

§§ `2.10 Guideline 10: When a Merger Involves Competing Buyers`

-- [pdf pages 27 and 28 of 51]: where a merger between employers may lessen competition for workers it "may lower wages or
-- slow wage growth, worsen benefits or working conditions, or result in other degradations of workplace quality. ...
-- evidence that a merger may have any one or more of these effects can demonstrate that substantial competition exists
-- between the merging firms."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.10 [pdf pages 27 and 28 of 51]
GIVEN effects IS A LIST OF `Effect on workers`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of these effects can demonstrate substantial competition between the merging employers` IF
    NOT (null effects)

-- [pdf page 28 of 51]: "a merger's harm to competition among buyers is not saved by benefits to competition among
-- sellers. ... If the parties claim any benefits to competition in a relevant buyer market, the Agencies will assess
-- those claims using the frameworks in Section 3." Section 3.3 ([pdf page 33 of 51]) likewise will not credit "benefits
-- outside the relevant market". This says only whether a claimed benefit is in the market where competition may be
-- lessened, so that the Agencies would assess it under section 3 at all. It weighs nothing, and its name should not be
-- read as saying a benefit offsets the harm: section 3.3 ([pdf page 33 of 51]) quotes the Supreme Court, "possible
-- economies [from a merger] cannot be used as a defense to illegality", and credits efficiencies only as showing that no
-- lessening of competition is threatened in the first place (fork G10-1).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 2.10 and 3.3 [pdf pages 28 and 33 of 51]
@desc Whether a claimed benefit is in the market where competition may be lessened; a benefit elsewhere does not save the harm. It is not a weighing of benefit against harm.
GIVEN `the market where competition may be lessened` IS A STRING
      `the market where the benefit arises` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the benefit may be weighed against the harm` IF
    `the market where the benefit arises` EQUALS `the market where competition may be lessened`

§§ `2.11 Guideline 11: When an Acquisition Involves Partial Ownership or Minority Interests`

-- Section 1 ([pdf page 4 of 51]): "The Agencies apply the frameworks in Guidelines 1-6 to assess if an acquisition of
-- partial control or common ownership may substantially lessen competition." 2.11 ([pdf pages 29 and 30 of 51]): the
-- Agencies have concerns with both cross-ownership and common ownership, and "generally focus on three principal
-- effects". There is no test of Guideline 11's own, so the kinds and the effects are reported, not weighed. Footnote 55's
-- "20% investment" describes a case and is not a threshold: nothing here uses it.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 1 and section 2.11 [pdf pages 4, 29 and 30 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Guideline 11 facts`
GIVETH A `Guideline 11 finding`
`the Agencies' reading under Guideline 11 of` MEANS `Guideline 11 finding` WITH
    `kinds of partial ownership involved` IS facts's `the kinds of partial ownership the acquisition involves`
    `Guideline 11 effects present` IS facts's `the effects of the partial acquisition the evidence shows`
    `the Agencies examine the partial acquisition under the frameworks of Guidelines 1 to 6` IS NOT (null (facts's `the kinds of partial ownership the acquisition involves`))
